meta {
  name: Mark the register
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/attendance/register
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-School-Id: {{schoolCode}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "subject_offering_id": "{{offeringId}}",
    "period_id": "{{periodOneId}}",
    "attendance_date": "2026-11-02",
    "source": "manual",
    "entries": [
      {
        "student_id": "{{studentId}}",
        "status": "present"
      }
    ]
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.created: eq 1
  res.body.updated: eq 0
  res.body.count: eq 1
  res.body.marks[0].status: eq present
  res.body.marks[0].timetable_slot_id: eq {{slotId}}
}

script:post-response {
  bru.setEnvVar("markId", res.body.marks[0].id);
}

docs {
  200, NOT 201, and it is deliberate. A register is not a resource being
  created; it is a whole class's marks being asserted, some new and some
  replaced, and the created and updated counts are the answer. There is no
  Location header because there is nothing single to point at.

  timetable_slot_id in the response was never sent. It, and teacher_id, are
  resolved from the occurrence standing on 2 November, which is what ties the
  mark to the class that was actually taught rather than to the class that
  was planned.

  source is manual, import or device, and defaults to manual. It is the field
  a biometric feed or a spreadsheet import sets so a later correction can tell
  a machine's guess from a teacher's judgement.

  The whole register is one transaction: if any entry cannot be reached, none
  of them is written. At most 400 entries.
}
